    "measurementTechnique": [
      "In July 2019, field-based active layer depth (ALD) measurements across the Teller and Mile 80\nsites were collected. We used a spatial sampling strategy to thoroughly capture the spatial\nvariability of tundra vegetation, permafrost features, and disturbances. At each sampling\nlocation, a thaw probe (130 cm long, 1 cm diameter, marked with 5 cm increments) was pushed into\nthe ground until resistance of the permafrost layer was encountered. To link with remote sensing\nimagery, the coordinates of each location were measured. Using the field observed ALD\nmeasurement in combination with spectral and topographic predictors derivatives from DJI UAS\nimagery (See dataset https://doi.org/10.5440/1906348), we used a spatially explicit RF\nregression model to predict and map ALD across our study landscapes. For comparison, the\nmodeling approach was also applied to AVIRIS-NG and Sentinel-2 datasets, in combination with the\nArctic-DEM. For more information about the methods, please refer to Hantson et al., 2024,\n\u201cScaling Arctic landscape and permafrost features improves active layer depth\nmodeling\u201d."
    ],
